      "claim": "Bumetanide, a structurally related sulfamoylbenzoic acid loop diuretic, has an absolute oral bioavailability of 70.4 ± 17.4% in healthy subjects, supporting high F for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Tolfenamic acid, an anthranilic acid with a carboxylic acid and halogenated aryl group, shows absolute oral bioavailability of 60.4% in healthy volunteers, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C20"],
      "claim": "Hydrochlorothiazide, a sulfonamide diuretic, has an absolute oral bioavailability of 60.2% in humans, supporting high F for this sulfamoyl-containing query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C33"],
      "claim": "Metolazone, a sulfonamide diuretic, is approximately 100% bioavailable orally in humans, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C35"],
      "claim": "Azosemide, a close thiophene analogue of furosemide, has absolute oral bioavailability of only ~20.4% in healthy fasting humans, providing some low-F evidence, but its high first-pass metabolism limits transfer to furosemide.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is furosemide, whose intrinsic oral bioavailability in humans is typically ~60% and well above the 20% threshold. Multiple structurally related sulfonamide/anthranilic acid diuretics and NSAIDs show high oral bioavailability, while the low-F azosemide analogue is less transferable due to its distinct first-pass metabolism. Therefore predict high.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct parent furosemide absolute bioavailability record was retrieved; the closest record (C06) is for furosemide acyl glucuronide, a metabolite, not the parent drug.",
    "No bioavailability data under cystic fibrosis conditions were retrieved; CF-related GI changes could alter absorption but are unlikely to drop F below 20%.",
    "Azosemide's low F is attributed to high first-pass metabolism, which may not apply to furosemide."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

